.TH "MACRO8X" "1" "12Apr96" "v1.0" "PDP/8 Assembler"

.SH NAME
macro8x \- a PDP/8 macro8x-like assembler

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macro8x
[ \-d \-m \-p \-r \-x ] inputfile inputfile...

.SH DESCRIPTION
This is a cross-assembler for PDP/8 assembly language programs. It produces
output files in bin format, rim format, and using appropriate pseudo-ops, a
combination of rim and bin formats. A listing file is always produced with an
optional symbol table and/or a symbol cross-reference (concordance). The
permanent symbol table can be output in a form that may be read back in to
create a customized permanent symbol table. Errors are output to a separate
file with the filename, line number, column number, and error message, as well
as marking the error in the listing file.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Dump the symbol table at the end of assembly.
.TP
.B \-m
Print macro expansions.
.TP
.B \-p
Generate a file with the permanent symbols in it.
.TP
.B \-r
Produce output in rim format (default is bin format).
.TP
.B \-x
Generate a cross-reference (concordance) of user symbols.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Assembler error diagnostics are output to an error file and inserted in the
listing file. Each line in the error file has the form:
.nf
<filename>(<line>:<col>) : error: <message> at Loc = <loc>
.fi
An example error message is:
.nf
bintst.pal(17:9) : error: undefined symbol "UNDEF" at Loc = 07616
.fi

.SH BUGS
Only a minimal effort has been made to keep the listing format similar to the
PAL-8 listing format. The operation of the conditional assembly pseudo-ops may
not function exactly as the DEC versions. The RIMPUNch and BINPUNch pseudo-ops
do not change the binary output file type specified on startup. Floating point
input does not generate values exactly as the DEC compiler does.

.SH BUILD AND INSTALLATION
This program has been built and successfully executed on:
.TP
Linux (80486 CPU) using gcc
.TP
RS/6000 (AIX 3.2.5)
.TP
Borland C++ version 3.1 (large memory model)
.TP
Borland C++ version 4.52 (large memory model)

.SH HISTORICAL NOTE
This assembler was written to support the fleet of PDP-8 systems used by the
Bay Area Rapid Transit System. As of early 1997, this includes about 40 PDP-8/E
systems driving the train destination signs in passenger stations.

